  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Our lengthy run of dry and mild weather \ncontinues until the second half of the week.\n\nMid and high clouds already cover most of PA, but there are some\nthinner patches. Clouds aren't any lower than 9kft. This should\nremain the case for today with almost no low clouds/cu. However,\nmore moisture is brought into the area from the southwest on\nMonday. A sprinkle isn't out of the question over the N Monday \nnight - much like the very small and brief shower they had two \nnights ago. We'll keep the forecast at 0 PoP since there is no \nrisk of 0.01\" accumulating. That isn't great news for northern\nPA where BFD only accumulated 0.85\" through all of Oct, and is \ncurrently on a run of 14 straight days without measurable \n(0.01\") rain.\n\nUpper level ridging builds back into the region for the first \nhalf of the week, helping to prolong our dry stretch and keeping\nhigh temperatures 10 to 15 degrees above normal for much of\nweek. Highs in the lower 70s are in store for all but the higher\nelevations on Wed.\n\nChances for widespread rainfall return for the second half of\nthe week. Wed night is the earliest anyone will see precip as a\ncomplex system develops over the middle of the country. There \nis still a lot of uncertainty regarding the evolution of the \nupper air pattern and phasing of energy aloft that will decide\nthe development and progression of this system. The more\nweighted/favored (per WPC) ECMWF solution yields only a brief \n12-18hr window of precip as the cold front moves through (longer\nin the eastern half of the CWA than over the west) Wed night \nand Thurs. Cold air under a sharp trough could make the first\npart of the weekend a little chilly and damp with instability \nshowers.",
